language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ kenken: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Fill an n by n grid with the digits 1 to n so that each row and each column contains each digit exactly once. The grid is divided into cages, each with a target number. The operation of each cage is not given: a cage of one cell holds its target; a cage of two cells may use addition, subtraction, multiplication or division (a division must be exact, so the larger digit divided by the smaller one gives the target); a larger cage uses either addition or multiplication. Digits may repeat within a cage.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `kenken.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/kenken.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `kenken_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py kenken.json
$   savilerow model.eprime kenken_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the size of the grid, which has n rows and n columns
given n : int
$ number of cages, derived from the data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given num_p : int
$ problem_1[c]: the target of cage c, cages indexed from 1 (the data's index 0 is 1)
given problem_1 : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_p)] of int
$ problem_2_len[c]: the number of cells of cage c, derived from the data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given problem_2_len : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_p)] of int
$ problem_2_dim2: the largest number of cells of any cage, derived from the data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given problem_2_dim2 : int
$ problem_2[c,k,1] and problem_2[c,k,2]: row and column (1-based, as in the data) of cell k of cage c; cells beyond problem_2_len[c] are padding and hold 0
given problem_2 : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_p), int(1..problem_2_dim2), int(1..2)] of int

$ the largest product of digits that a cage can have: n to the power of the largest cage size
letting largest_product = n ** problem_2_dim2

$ x[r,c]: the digit in row r and column c of the grid
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ cell_val[c,k]: the digit in cell k of cage c (padding cells are fixed to 1, so that a product ignores them)
find cell_val : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_p), int(1..problem_2_dim2)] of int(1..n)
$ cage_prod[c,k]: the product of the first k digits of cage c (cage_prod[c,0] is the empty product, 1)
find cage_prod : matrix indexed by [int(1..num_p), int(0..problem_2_dim2)] of int(1..largest_product)

such that
$ each row of the grid contains each digit exactly once
forAll r : int(1..n) . allDiff([x[r,c] | c : int(1..n)]),
$ each column of the grid contains each digit exactly once
forAll c : int(1..n) . allDiff([x[r,c] | r : int(1..n)]),
$ each cell of each cage takes the digit of the grid cell it names
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . forAll k : int(1..problem_2_dim2) . forAll r : int(1..n) . forAll col : int(1..n) . (((k <= problem_2_len[c]) /\ (problem_2[c,k,1] = r) /\ (problem_2[c,k,2] = col)) -> (cell_val[c,k] = x[r,col])),
$ padding cells of a cage are fixed to 1
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . forAll k : int(1..problem_2_dim2) . ((k > problem_2_len[c]) -> (cell_val[c,k] = 1)),
$ a cage of one cell holds its target
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . ((problem_2_len[c] = 1) -> (cell_val[c,1] = problem_1[c])),
$ a cage of two cells: the two digits add to, multiply to, differ by, or divide exactly to give the target
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . ((problem_2_len[c] = 2) -> (((cell_val[c,1] + cell_val[c,2]) = problem_1[c]) \/ ((cell_val[c,1] * cell_val[c,2]) = problem_1[c]) \/ ((cell_val[c,1] - cell_val[c,2]) = problem_1[c]) \/ ((cell_val[c,2] - cell_val[c,1]) = problem_1[c]) \/ (cell_val[c,1] = (problem_1[c] * cell_val[c,2])) \/ (cell_val[c,2] = (problem_1[c] * cell_val[c,1])))),
$ the cumulative product: each cage's product includes its cells, the empty product is 1
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . (cage_prod[c,0] = 1),
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . forAll k : int(1..problem_2_dim2) . (cage_prod[c,k] = (cage_prod[c,k-1] * cell_val[c,k])),
$ a larger cage: its digits add up to the target, or multiply to it
forAll c : int(1..num_p) . ((problem_2_len[c] >= 3) -> (((sum k : int(1..problem_2_dim2) . toInt(k <= problem_2_len[c]) * cell_val[c,k]) = problem_1[c]) \/ (cage_prod[c,problem_2_dim2] = problem_1[c])))
